  "overview": {
    "text": "Volume, commonly symbolized V, measures the three-dimensional space occupied by matter or bounded by a surface; unlike area, it is expressed in cubic units. Its SI unit is the cubic meter (m³), while a liter (L) equals a cubic decimeter and a milliliter equals a cubic centimeter, so the same unit system applies to solids, liquids, and gases. In thermodynamics, V describes the space occupied by a system such as a gas: it appears with pressure and temperature in the ideal-gas relation, and a change in gas volume can accompany pressure–volume work. Thus, volume connects everyday measurement with geometry and the analysis of physical systems.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://www.nist.gov/pml/owm/si-units-volume",
      "https://www.nist.gov/pml/special-publication-811/nist-guide-si-chapter-8",
      "https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/12-2-first-law-of-thermodynamics-thermal-energy-and-work"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "Volume measures three-dimensional space and is commonly represented by V.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.nist.gov/pml/owm/si-units-volume"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The SI unit is the cubic meter (m³); it can express the volume of a solid, liquid, or gas.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.nist.gov/pml/special-publication-811/nist-guide-si-chapter-8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "One liter equals one cubic decimeter (1 L = 1 dm³), and one milliliter equals one cubic centimeter (1 mL = 1 cm³).",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.nist.gov/pml/owm/si-units-volume"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A cube measuring 10 cm on each side has a volume of 1 L.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.nist.gov/pml/owm/si-units-volume"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "For an ideal gas, pressure, volume, and temperature are related; changing a gas’s volume can also involve pressure–volume work.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/12-2-first-law-of-thermodynamics-thermal-energy-and-work"
      ]
    }
  ],
